{"title":"Side","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Overview\">Overview</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Side&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Overview\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A <b>side</b> in Euclidean geometry is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line_segment\" title=\"Line segment\">line segment</a> that connects two vertices of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Shape&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Shape (page does not exist)\">shape</a>. In shapes, sides are always connected. The number of sides on a shape depends on the number of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Angle\" title=\"Angle\">angles</a> because the number of angles is the number of sides. \"Side\" is also be used as a general term in three-dimensional geometry where side refers to a face of an object.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Side&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018050857\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.007 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.011 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 8/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 253/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 0/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.314      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.314      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:32189:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018050857 and revisio